// @expect-error Struct 'Outer' contains itself: field 'nested' of 'Outer' has type 'Inner' and field 'back' of 'Inner' has type 'Outer'. A struct keeps its fields inside itself, so every 'Outer' would contain another 'Outer' without end. Make 'Inner' a class, as in 'class Inner { ... }' where module 'inner' declares it, because a class field only refers to another object.
// @expect-error Struct 'Inner' contains itself: field 'back' of 'Inner' has type 'Outer' and field 'nested' of 'Outer' has type 'Inner'. A struct keeps its fields inside itself, so every 'Inner' would contain another 'Inner' without end. Make 'Outer' a class, as in 'class Outer { ... }' where module 'main' declares it, because a class field only refers to another object.
// A by-value struct cycle is impossible to lay out even when it crosses a
// module boundary.
import Inner from inner;

public struct Outer {
    Inner nested;
    long tail = 0;
}

main() -> int {
    return 0;
}
